      <div class="deck-notes__head">
        <span>Presenter notes</span>
        <span><kbd>S</kbd> close · <kbd>P</kbd> presenter view</span>
      </div>
      <div class="deck-notes__body" data-notes-body>
        Notes for the current slide appear here.
      </div>
    </aside>

    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="slides">
        <!-- 1 ───────────────────────────── HERO (eyebrow + big headline) -->
        <section class="hero" data-section="Opening">
          <span class="eyebrow">Capability Showcase · demo content only</span>
          <h1 class="h-set">
            <span class="h-line"><span>EVERY SLIDE</span></span>
            <span class="h-line"><span class="accent">KIND, ONCE.</span></span>
          </h1>
          <p>A reference gallery, not a talk. Every word here is placeholder.</p>
          <p class="byline">Reference deck · atilileri.github.io/decks/showcase-for-asml</p>
          <aside class="notes">
            This is the capability gallery, not a presentation. Every headline,
            number and code sample is demo filler — the point is the slide kind
            underneath it. The real deck is /decks/asml-ai. Press S for these
            inline notes; P opens the separate presenter window.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 2 ───────────────────── CHAPTER divider (triggers chapter-wash) -->
        <section class="chapter" data-section="01 — Telling a story" data-chapter>
          <span class="chapter-no">CHAPTER 01</span>
          <h2>Slides that<br />carry a narrative.</h2>
          <aside class="notes">
            Slide kind: CHAPTER DIVIDER. A signal-blue panel sweeps the screen on
            entry. One of the three cinematic devices — worth 2–3× in a real talk,
            no more. Headline text is placeholder.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 3 ───────────────────── SCROLL-DRIVEN NARRATIVE (in-slide overflow) -->
        <section class="scrolly" data-section="01 — Telling a story" data-scrolly>
          <span class="eyebrow">Scroll-driven narrative</span>
          <div class="scroll-hint">&darr; scroll inside this slide</div>
          <div class="scroll-frame" data-scroll-frame>
            <div class="scroll-step" data-scroll-step>
              <h3>One slide, several beats.</h3>
              <p>
                Placeholder beat one. A scrolly slide holds an argument that needs
                room to unfold, without spending four slides on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="scroll-step" data-scroll-step>
              <h3>The active beat brightens.</h3>
              <p>
                Placeholder beat two. Whatever is centred holds full contrast; the
                beats above and below dim back out of the way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="scroll-step" data-scroll-step>
              <h3>The room reads at your pace.</h3>
              <p>
                Placeholder beat three. You control the scroll, so the reveal stays
